<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="volatile benzenoid biosynthesis I (ester formation)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4203" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; A broad variety of volatile ester compounds have been found to constitute components of complex cocktails in numerous plants emitted to serve in both reproductive and vegetative processes. More than 1.000 low-molecular-weight organic compounds have been identified to date |CITS: [15326281]|, most of them components of floral scents. The flower volatiles function as pollinator attractants but may also be involved in defense mechanism, such as airborne signals to attract natural predators of herbivores |CITS: [12732318]|, to directly repel herbivores |CITS: [Gershenzon91]| or to activate defense-related genes in neighboring plants or healthy tissue in infected plants |CITS: [Shulaev97]|. Methylbenzoate, methylsalicylate, benzylbenzoate and benzylacetate are found and investigated in floral scents of |FRAME:TAX-36903| and &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. Together with cinnamoic esters, terpenoids, fatty acid derivatives, C5-branched compounds, and various nitrogen and sulfur containing compounds |CITS: [15326281]| benzenoid esters form the core of physiologically active volatiles in plants. Certain volatiles such as methylsalicylate and methyl jasmonate (|FRAME:PWY-735|) are also thought to be involved in cell-to-cell signaling |CITS: [Shulaev97]|. &lt;b&gt;Special information&lt;/b&gt;: The acetyl CoA:acetyl alcohol acetyltransferase (BEAT) catalyzes the formation of benzylacetate in |FRAME:TAX-36903| which represents 20 to 40% of the total scent output of this plant. Two other major volatiles benzylbenzoate and methylsalicylate account for 5% each of the scent |CITS: [9489012]|. In &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; the emission of methylsalicylate and methylbenzoate has been observed as a response to biotic and abiotic stress. The enzyme(s) catalyzing those reactions &lt;b&gt;SA&lt;/b&gt;MT (salicylic acid methyltransferase) and &lt;b&gt;BA&lt;/b&gt;MT (benzoic acid methyltransferase) - together with the &lt;b&gt;TH&lt;/b&gt;eobromine synthase termed as &lt;b&gt;SABATH&lt;/b&gt; family - represent a structural related group with no significant sequence similarity to other methyltransferases |CITS: [DAURIA03]|. The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; gene AtBSMT1 and its ortholog in |FRAME:TAX-59689| (AlBSMT1) can use both substrates (salicylic acid, benzoic acid) but express different substrate affinities |CITS: [14617060]|. Benzoyl-coenzyme A (CoA):benzyl alcohol benzoyl transferase (BEBT) identified from |FRAME:TAX-36903| catalyzes the formation of benzylbenzoate (a major volatile) with a broader substrate specificity towards other alcohols and acetyl CoAs. Interestingly, the most similar protein to BEBT found in Arabidopsis does not accept benzoyl CoA but catalyzes the formation of cis-3-hexen-1-yl acetate, another green-leaf volatile using acetyl CoA as substrate |CITS: [12226525]|.</Description>
